WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.943
Temperature and water, and how they vary through the year, largely decide which

2
00:00:07.943 --> 00:00:09.083
biome a place supports.

3
00:00:10.003 --> 00:00:15.203
A biome is a large region with similar climate and dominant vegetation and animals.

4
00:00:16.423 --> 00:00:19.143
Tropical wet forest fits warmth and rain in every month.

5
00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:21.183
Desert fits scarce rain.

6
00:00:21.943 --> 00:00:24.843
So climate gives us a first prediction of the vegetation.

7
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:28.943
We can compare that prediction with what actually grows there.

8
00:00:29.623 --> 00:00:31.403
But climate does not fix the biome.

9
00:00:31.403 --> 00:00:34.243
Fire and soils modify the pattern.

10
00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:39.707
Under the same rain, soils differ in water holding capacity and nutrients.

11
00:00:40.627 --> 00:00:42.347
That can support different vegetation.

12
00:00:43.487 --> 00:00:48.067
Staver and colleagues analysed tropical tree cover in Africa, Australia and South

13
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:53.847
America. Across a wide rainfall range with a short dry season, places were mostly

14
00:00:53.847 --> 00:00:54.967
savanna or forest.

15
00:00:56.187 --> 00:00:58.547
Fire was associated with keeping areas open.

16
00:00:59.327 --> 00:01:02.967
A changing climate also need not mean an immediate change of biome.

17
00:01:03.527 --> 00:01:06.687
That depends on dispersal, soils, fire and time.

18
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.380
Where does that climate come from?

19
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.040
Sunlight striking Earth at a slant spreads over more ground.

20
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.420
Over the year, the tropics receive more energy and the poles less.

21
00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.640
The atmosphere and oceans carry heat poleward.

22
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.160
Near the equator, warm moist air rises, cools and drops rain.

23
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.180
Dry air flows poleward aloft and sinks in the subtropics.

24
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.020
It warms as it sinks, suppressing rain.

25
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.620
This is the idealized Hadley cell.

26
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.300
It helps explain wet equatorial regions and subtropical deserts.

27
00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:43.080
Mountains add wet windward slopes and dry rain shadows.

28
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.370
Annual rainfall totals hide timing.

29
00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.450
Knapp and colleagues used shelters in a native grassland and reapplied rain.

30
00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.470
They kept the total constant but made events fewer and larger.

31
00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:00.990
Soil moisture became more variable, plant production fell, and species diversity

32
00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:04.390
rose. That result applies to that grassland.

33
00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.230
Repeat it elsewhere before generalizing.

34
00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:12.490
Compare unsheltered plots with sheltered plots receiving the natural pattern, too.

35
00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:16.290
That checks whether the shelters themselves change the result.

36
00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:20.593
Organisms also experience local microclimates.

37
00:02:21.533 --> 00:02:26.193
Beneath forest canopies, understories were cooler on average and in their hottest

38
00:02:26.193 --> 00:02:28.413
temperatures than nearby open habitat.

39
00:02:29.313 --> 00:02:32.233
In cold conditions, canopies warmed the understory.

40
00:02:32.993 --> 00:02:36.713
The temperature an organism experiences is not the station's.

41
00:02:37.573 --> 00:02:38.993
Climate predicts biomes roughly.

42
00:02:40.153 --> 00:02:42.173
Local conditions modify that prediction.

43
00:02:43.233 --> 00:02:47.113
A smaller point: a climate diagram helps read the seasonal pattern.

44
00:02:47.913 --> 00:02:51.233
The lesson's made-up station stays warm and humid all year.

45
00:02:52.053 --> 00:02:55.373
Its tropical rainforest climate fits tropical wet forest.

46
00:02:56.173 --> 00:03:00.113
That is a hypothesis to test against the vegetation actually present.
